# Getting started

Odroe is currently developed from source. Keep the source checkout and the
Flutter application beside each other while the public package is prepared.

## Create the product

From their shared parent directory:

```sh
git clone https://github.com/odroe/odroe.git
flutter create --empty --platforms=android,ios,web my_app
cd my_app
```

Skip the clone when the Odroe checkout already exists. `--empty` is deliberate:
the initializer refuses to replace Flutter's counter example or any custom Dart
source.

Add the sibling checkout as a direct application dependency:

```yaml
dependencies:
  odroe:
    path: ../odroe
```

Resolve it, then initialize the product:

```sh
flutter pub get
dart run odroe init
```

`init` verifies a Flutter application, its direct Odroe dependency, and the
resolved checkout before writing anything. It creates only this starter:

```text
lib/
├── main.dart
├── routes/
│   ├── route.dart
│   └── page.dart
├── routes.dart          # generated client tree
└── routes.server.dart   # generated server tree
```

Running `init` again makes no changes. A custom `main.dart`, route, generated
target without Odroe ownership, or Dart test causes the complete operation to
fail without partial writes. There is no `--force`; `init --project <dir>` is
available for automation.

## Customize the shared route

`lib/routes/route.dart` owns the platform-neutral route and its semantic
Document. The starter is immediately useful to a browser, crawler, or native
server without importing Flutter.

```dart
import 'package:odroe/document.dart';
import 'package:odroe/router.dart';

final route =
    AppRoute<NoParams, NoSearch, NoData>(
      metadata: const RouteMetadata(
        title: 'Odroe App',
        description: 'A product built with Odroe.',
      ),
    ).document(
      (_) => const RouteDocument(
        language: 'en',
        body: HtmlElement(
          'main',
          children: <HtmlNode>[
            HtmlElement(
              'h1',
              children: <HtmlNode>[HtmlText('Hello from Odroe')],
            ),
          ],
        ),
      ),
    );
```

Each directory becomes its URL. For example,
`lib/routes/about/route.dart` maps to `/about`.

## Customize the Flutter page

`lib/routes/page.dart` projects the same route into Flutter:

```dart
import 'package:flutter/material.dart';
import 'package:odroe/router_flutter.dart';

import 'route.dart' as definition;

final route = definition.route.page(
  build: (_) => const Scaffold(
    body: Center(child: Text('Hello from Odroe')),
  ),
);
```

The initialized `lib/main.dart` already installs `DocumentModule` before
`RouterModule` and returns `MaterialApp.router`. Add Query, RPC, or other
product modules only when the application uses them.

## Develop

Run the semantic server alone:

```sh
dart run odroe dev --server-only
```

Development serves files directly from `public/` and never reuses stale
`build/web` HTML. For Flutter, pass Flutter arguments after `--`:

```sh
flutter devices
dart run odroe dev -- -d chrome
dart run odroe dev -- -d <ios-device-id>
```

Replace `<ios-device-id>` with the identifier reported by Flutter. `dev` and
`build` regenerate routes automatically; `dart run odroe generate` remains a
fast explicit compiler check.

## Build

Choose the artifact intentionally:

```sh
# Flutter Web plus prerendered assets, without a deployable server
dart run odroe build --no-server web

# Flutter Web plus the default native server artifact
dart run odroe build web

# Client-only Android APK
dart run odroe build --no-server apk

# Native server only
dart run odroe build --server-only
```

`--no-server` skips only the deployable server artifact. Odroe still runs the
generated Dart server source to prerender known locations into `build/web`.
Because this starter contains `page.dart`, an assets-only build must name a
Flutter target such as `web`; a Document-only product can use bare
`dart run odroe build --no-server`.

Known locations come from static file routes and `lib/prerender.dart`; link
crawling is disabled by default. Add `--prerender-crawl` only when page links
should discover additional same-origin HTML. See [Deployment](/docs/deploy)
for budgets, static-root behavior, and platform targets.
